Identifying the exploited script allows security teams to understand exactly which vulnerability was used in the attack. This knowledge is critical for finding the appropriate patch or workaround to close the security gap and prevent future exploitation. Can you provide the vulnerable PHP script name?

Establishing the timeline of an attack, starting from the initial exploitation attempt, what is the complete request URI of the first SQLi attempt by the attacker?
An SQLi attack would have to have "/search.php" in the URL because you're searching for something initially. The first search we see is for "harry potter" in packet 88, which has no indications of SQLi. However, looking a bit further in our search query, we come across packet 357, which shows a query parameter of "search=book%20and%201=1". Therefore, the entire request URI (decoded with https://www.url-encode-decode.com/) is:
/search.php?search=book and 1=1; -- -

Can you provide the complete request URI that was used to read the web server's available databases?
This one took some research for me. Thankfully, I found this link:
https://www.devart.com/dbforge/mysql/studio/how-to-show-all-database-list-in-mysql.html
Which mentions:
List databases in MySQL by information_schema
[...]
SELECT schema_name
FROM information_schema.schemata;
Searching for "information_schema.schemata" in Wireshark reveals packet 1520, where we see:
http://bookworldstore.com/search.php?search=book%27%20UNION%20ALL%20SELECT%20NULL%2CCONCAT%280x7178766271%2CJSON_ARRAYAGG%28CONCAT_WS%280x7a76676a636b%2Cschema_name%29%29%2C0x7176706a71%29%20FROM%20INFORMATION_SCHEMA.SCHEMATA--%20-
And decoded, that is:
/search.php?search=book' UNION ALL SELECT NULL,CONCAT(0x7178766271,JSON_ARRAYAGG(CONCAT_WS(0x7a76676a636b,schema_name)),0x7176706a71) FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.SCHEMATA-- -

Assessing the impact of the breach and data access is crucial, including the potential harm to the organization's reputation. What's the table name containing the website users data?
Continuing past that packet and finding to the next search, packet 1548 shows the user querying table "626f6f6b776f726c645f6462." This translates to "bookworld_db", so this is likely not the user data table. The next several searches are within that table, but eventually packet 1612 reveals:
search=book' UNION ALL SELECT NULL,CONCAT(0x7178766271,JSON_ARRAYAGG(CONCAT_WS(0x7a76676a636b,column_name,column_type)),0x7176706a71) FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS WHERE table_name=0x637573746f6d657273 AND table_schema=0x626f6f6b776f726c645f6462-- -
The table_name looks to be encoded in hex. Translated, it says "customers".

The website directories hidden from the public could serve as an unauthorized access point or contain sensitive functionalities not intended for public access. Can you provide the name of the directory discovered by the attacker?
A few packets before the previous answer, using the same method (translating the URL and then the hex of the table name), a table called "admin" is discovered.

Knowing which credentials were used allows us to determine the extent of account compromise. What are the credentials used by the attacker for logging in?
Start by searching for "username=admin" in Wireshark. The last login request will likely be the successful one. This leads to packet 88699, which shows the credentials of admin:admin123!

We need to determine if the attacker gained further access or control of our web server. What's the name of the malicious script uploaded by the attacker?
It has to be after that packet, and it has to be an HTTP POST. The very next POST packet is 88757, and analyzing the packet bytes, the answer is uncovered:
form-data;name="fileToUpload";filename="NVri2vhp.php"
